@startuml alert-scope-filtering-classes
title Shared Scope Predicate: One Definition, Two Callers (CP2 / future CP3)

skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam classBorderColor #888888
skinparam classBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam classHeaderBackgroundColor #DBEAFE

class AlertRow <<alerts table row>> {
  alert_id: string
  entity_id: string
  entity_type: string
  alert_type: string
  payload: JSONB
}

class GeoBounds {
  min_lat: number
  max_lat: number
  min_lon: number
  max_lon: number
}

class ScopeFilter <<the shared predicate's one input shape>> {
  bounds: GeoBounds
  entity_types: string[] | null
  alert_types: string[] | null
}

note right of ScopeFilter
  null on entity_types/alert_types means
  "no restriction" -- used only by demo's
  ad-hoc bbox filter, which has no saved
  entity/alert-type dimension to draw from.
  An operator's ScopeFilter always carries
  concrete arrays from their saved scope.
end note

class "extractAlertPosition()" as Extract <<pure function>> {
  SIGNAL_LOSS -> payload.last_known_lat/lon
  UNSCHEDULED_PROXIMITY -> payload.lat/lon
  COMPOSITE -> payload.proximity.lat/lon
  ROUTE_DEVIATION -> null (undecided, fail closed)
}

class "matchesScope()" as Predicate <<pure function, one definition>> {
  (alert: AlertRow, scope: ScopeFilter) => boolean
}

class "GET /alerts (CP2)" as RestCaller <<REST, this checkpoint>> {
  builds ScopeFilter from saved\nuser_workspaces row, or\nfrom a demo bbox query param
}

class "alert-events fan-out (future CP3)" as WsCaller <<WebSocket, not yet built>> {
  builds ScopeFilter from the same\nsaved user_workspaces row,\nheld in the connection map
}

AlertRow --> Extract : input
Extract --> Predicate : position (or null -> excluded)
GeoBounds *-- ScopeFilter
ScopeFilter --> Predicate : input
RestCaller ..> Predicate : calls
WsCaller ..> Predicate : will call

note bottom of Predicate
  One function, tested once. REST and the
  future WebSocket path can never disagree
  on what counts as "in scope" because
  neither reimplements the check.
end note

@enduml
